    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Developer basics</p>
    <h1>What Is a Computer Date Number? A Plain Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">Many computers store a date as one large number: the count of seconds since 1 January 1970. This guide explains that number and why you might see it.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Examples</h2>
<ul>
<li>0 is 1 January 1970 at 00:00:00 UTC, the start of the epoch.</li>
<li>1,000,000,000 is 9 September 2001 at 01:46:40 UTC.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Seconds or milliseconds?</h2>
<p>Many systems use seconds, but JavaScript and some databases use milliseconds. A millisecond value looks about a thousand times larger. If a date shows as the year 50000, the value is probably in milliseconds.</p>
<h2>Time zones</h2>
<p>A Unix timestamp is always in UTC, so it does not change with time zones. Convert it to local time only when you display it to a person.</p>
<h2>The 2038 limit</h2>
<p>Systems that store the timestamp in a signed 32-bit integer run out at 2,147,483,647, which is 19 January 2038 at 03:14:07 UTC. Modern systems use 64-bit numbers, which avoid the limit.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/unix-timestamp-converter.html">Computer Date Converter</a> turns a timestamp into a date and a date into a timestamp, with times shown in UTC.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>The timestamp 1,000,000,000 is 9 September 2001 at 01:46:40 UTC. The timestamp 1,793,004,000 is 26 October 2026 at 08:40 UTC. Both count seconds from the start of 1 January 1970.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>How do I tell seconds from milliseconds?</summary><p>A seconds value for a current date has 10 digits, such as 1793004000. A milliseconds value has 13 digits, such as 1793004000000.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why does UTC matter?</summary><p>UTC has no daylight saving changes, so the same timestamp always means the same moment. Local time depends on the time zone.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is the 2038 problem?</summary><p>Systems that store the timestamp in a signed 32-bit number will overflow after 19 January 2038 at 03:14:07 UTC. Modern systems use 64-bit values.</p></details>
      </section>
